WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:02.110
- [Narrator] Traffic to the moon

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:04.140
is going to pick up this year.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.310
- We think it'll be very exciting

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.120
to have a base on the moon.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.710
- [Narrator] Space
transportation companies

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:13.340
from SpaceX to Blue Origin
are teaming up with NASA.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.920
- The moon also needs infrastructure.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.170
Lemme show you something.

00:00:19.409 --> 00:00:21.320
(dramatic music)

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.153
(audience cheering)

00:00:22.153 --> 00:00:22.986
- [Narrator] And they have their sights

00:00:22.986 --> 00:00:24.540
on a valuable resource.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.980
- Hundreds of millions
of tons of water ice

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.950
at the poles of the moon.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.540
- [Narrator] Some geological analytics say

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:31.850
the market for space water

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.200
will be worth $206 billion
over the next 30 years.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.300
- Water is the oil of space.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.280
- [Narrator] This would allow rockets

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.860
to move deeper into the cosmos

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.940
and eventually go to Mars.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.963
So how do NASA and its partner
companies plan to do this?

00:00:47.675 --> 00:00:48.820
- [Announcer] Three, two.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.210
- [Narrator] First,
you've got to understand

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:53.250
that it takes a lot of
fuel to go to space.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.140
- [George] The moon rockets in the 1960s

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.680
were mostly just giant fuel tanks

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.070
because all the fuel it ever needs has

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:03.160
to be brought from the Earth.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.660
- [Narrator] George Sowers is
a professor of space resources

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.680
at Colorado School of Mines

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.150
and he's been enlisted by NASA

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.783
to research water ice mining technology.

00:01:11.783 --> 00:01:16.640
- The importance of having
sources of fuel beyond Earth

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.670
is due to what we call the
tyranny of the rocket equation.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.104
What the rocket equation shows

00:01:22.104 --> 00:01:24.270
is that as you increase the amount

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.190
of velocity that you want,

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.900
the amount of propellant that you need

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.454
to deliver that increases exponentially.

00:01:31.454 --> 00:01:32.287
- [Narrator] It turns out,

00:01:32.287 --> 00:01:33.650
the fuel itself isn't expensive.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:38.650
- The cost of propellant
is actually only about .3%

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.500
of the cost of the rocket or of emission.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.110
So if emission costs $60 million,

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.570
the cost of propellant is only $200,000.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:49.020
- [Narrator] What is expensive
is the weight of the fuel

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.070
because you need a lot of it

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.240
to break through Earth's
gravitational grip into space.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.610
- Moving mass from Earth into space

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.380
is really, really hard to do.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.340
It requires big rockets

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.033
and harnessing power is very expensive.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:04.953
- [Narrator] So one way

00:02:04.953 --> 00:02:06.680
to reduce the weight launch from Earth,

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.823
build a refueling station in space.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.120
According to Sower's projection,

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.940
refueling in space cuts the cost

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.920
of every mission by at least 10 to 20%.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.460
Also, if you can refuel
in space at least once,

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.423
the rocket can travel farther
and at greater velocity.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.070
This concept is already an important part

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.773
of SpaceX's Starship design.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.590
- It can get tankered to
fill up its propellant tanks

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:35.460
and so it can depart from lowest orbit

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.140
with 1,200 tons of propellant.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.010
Your delta velocity is
enough to transport 350 tons

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.560
to the surface of the moon or Mars.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.370
In practicing docking
with the Space Station,

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.220
the SpaceX has also
learnt how to rendezvous

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.523
and dock in orbit in
a complex environment.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.210
- [Narrator] Because
of SpaceX's experience,

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.570
NASA has chosen it to help
develop orbital refueling.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.200
But to make refueling sustainable,

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.330
the Space Agency is
looking for alternatives

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.274
to transporting fuel from Earth.

00:03:06.274 --> 00:03:10.120
- In space resources, we have to use them.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.060
- [Narrator] Enter space water.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.340
- [Jeff] We were given a gift,

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.340
this nearby body called the moon.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:16.440
There's water there.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.540
It's in the form of ice.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.270
- [Narrator] Jeff Bezos's Blue Origin team

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:23.474
has a nearly $580 million
contract from NASA

00:03:23.474 --> 00:03:27.000
to design and develop a lunar
lander called the Blue Moon,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.370
which will deliver crew and cargo.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.850
This lander will be
powered by liquid hydrogen,

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:33.860
today's most common rocket fuel.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.760
- Ultimately, we're gonna
be able to get hydrogen

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.650
from that water on the moon

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.010
and be able to refuel these vehicles

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:43.370
on the surface of the moon and use them.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:44.600
- [Narrator] By running
an electric current

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:45.500
through the water,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.170
it can be split into hydrogen and oxygen.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.450
When the two gases are
combined, then ignited,

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.690
they explode, generating energy,

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.030
which then becomes propellant.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.370
To exploit water on the moon,

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.270
NASA has created a five-year plan

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.230
with international and commercial partners

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.310
to set up mining operations.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.373
- If it can be harvested and utilized,

00:04:04.373 --> 00:04:05.570
it's a game-changer.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.350
It's a paradigm shift.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.770
- [Narrator] Thomas Zurbuchen is the head

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:09.860
of science at NASA.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.010
He oversees the moon and
Mars research programs.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.531
- [Thomas] Water is an
integral part of all missions,

00:04:15.531 --> 00:04:17.820
just for sustaining life

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:18.873
and also for fuel.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:20.750
- [Narrator] So starting this year,

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:22.750
two commercial landers
will arrive on the moon,

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:24.910
delivering spectrometers
to map the location

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:28.230
of water in craters and
measure its concentration.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.490
- Next year, we're
flying the first attempt

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.000
at a mining drill to
the surface of the moon.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.030
It's called PRIME-1.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:38.300
We're gonna either modify
that we learnt something

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:39.430
or can work as a copy.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.630
We're also gonna fly on Viper.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:43.960
- [Narrator] Viper is a rover built

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.520
to explore the darker
regions of the South Pole.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.770
It will search for water ice
and start drilling for samples.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.900
By 2024, NASA expects
its first lunar outpost

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.440
called the Gateway will
be under construction.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.220
This will serve as a port for landers

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.670
en route to the lunar surface

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:01.730
and spacecraft headed to Mars and beyond.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:03.340
So if things go as planned,

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:06.530
by 2025, NASA can contract
robotics companies

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:07.690
to start mining

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:10.860
and three years later, begin
water production on the moon.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.610
- That surface ice is
gonna be the lowest cost

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:15.200
and the easiest to get.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.619
We can get at it by just heating it up

00:05:17.619 --> 00:05:19.940
and sublimating it into vapor

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.333
and capturing that vapor.

00:05:23.234 --> 00:05:24.770
(dramatic music)

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:25.860
- As we go forward,

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.500
there may be a time
where such a fuel depot

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.040
is absolutely critical for what we can do.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.730
Like if we wanna go down
to the moon multiple times

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:38.160
from the Gateway or from other locations,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.530
kind of being able to pick up fuel

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.390
is an enabling part.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:44.100
- [Narrator] Meanwhile,
there are other experiments

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.040
to create propellant in space.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.480
NASA's Perseverance rover

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.210
that landed on Mars in February

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:51.360
is testing a system to extract oxygen

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.330
from the carbon dioxide-rich atmosphere

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.390
to eventually make propellant.

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.210
And a recent study by
Purdue University confirms

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.700
the presence of widespread
ice beneath the surface

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.720
of Mars Arcadia Planitia's region,

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.590
potentially a source for making fuel

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.520
and supporting life.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.740
While most scientists say
a manned mission to Mars

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:09.927
is still a decade away,

00:06:09.927 --> 00:06:13.420
for now, practicing how
to use space resources

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:14.757
will be one of many key steps

00:06:14.757 --> 00:06:18.748
for humans to eventually
set foot on another planet.

00:06:18.748 --> 00:06:21.175
(pensive music)

